+# MetalSim: deterministic-mode CCD (Warp DeterministicMode.RUN_TO_RUN). Warp runs a kernel whose atomic_add
+# return values are consumed (slot counters) twice: phase 0 with every global array store suppressed and each
+# counter call returning 0, then a sort / scan of the recorded calls, then phase 1 with the real slots. ccd_kernel
+# consumes two counters (nccd for its EPA scratch row, nacon in write_contact) and EPA / multicontact write the
+# polytope into that scratch row and read it back, so in phase 0 every thread runs EPA on row 0 with its stores
+# dropped, reading stale memory, and the number of write_contact calls it records can differ from the number it
+# makes in phase 1. The slots then no longer match the calls: a contact lands in the slot reserved by another
+# thread (another world), leaving holes nowhere and the overflow flag clear. Seen as 2 of 256 stacked-box worlds
+# diverging at the first box-box contact (MetalSim research_notes/.../deterministic_ccd_divergence.md).
+#
+# In deterministic mode convex_narrowphase therefore runs CCD as two kernels: a solve kernel with no slot counter
+# (EPA / multicontact scratch row = thread id, launch width <= naccdmax, so the kernel is single phase and its
+# stores are real) that stages each collision's contacts, and a write kernel whose write_contact calls depend only
+# on the staged count, so its two phases make the same calls. The default (non-deterministic) path is unchanged.
